                <div class="ow-prose">

                    <p>
                        An office worker may wake up, travel through
                        traffic, arrive at work, sit through meetings,
                        spend hours answering emails, work on reports,
                        attend another meeting, return to a vehicle or
                        public transport and spend another long period
                        seated before getting home.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        This means that a person's daily physical
                        experience is not defined by the desk alone.
                        <strong>Commuting, workstation setup, screen
                        use, movement, workload, breaks, sleep and
                        recovery</strong> all form part of the wider
                        wellness picture.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Office worker wellness is therefore not about
                        telling employees to “sit properly” and
                        returning them to the same chair for another
                        five hours. It is about creating a pattern in
                        which work is supported by sensible setup,
                        regular movement and appropriate recovery.
                    </p>

